    "parent_path": "/explore/business/online-services/",
    "entity_type": "organization",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": "https://www.sheypoor.com/",
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": ["s1"],
    "description": "Sheypoor is an Iranian classified-advertising platform covering goods, property, vehicles, employment, and services. Its published interfaces combine local search with advertiser tools, while shareholder reporting describes a business model that includes paid additions to the classifieds service. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Sheypoor", "Online classifieds", "Local commerce", "Property listings", "Small businesses"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T19:05:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T19:05:02Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Sheypoor: Classified Advertising and Local Markets in Iran",
      "description": "Understand Sheypoor’s classifieds categories, advertiser workflow, regional presentation, and the limits of published business metrics."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "platform-overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A broad classifieds marketplace",
        "content": "Sheypoor presents itself as a platform for advertising goods and services through a website and application. Its main categories include vehicles, property, employment, household goods, electronics, personal items, and business services. The range places individual possessions alongside commercial offers and recruitment: the common feature is an advertisement connecting interested parties, rather than a uniform product sold by Sheypoor itself. [s1]\n\nFor readers unfamiliar with Iranian digital commerce, this distinction is useful. A property listing, a vacancy, and an advertisement for a household item all support discovery, but each leaves different questions to be resolved between participants. The platform’s breadth should therefore be understood as a shared advertising infrastructure, not evidence that every category follows the same purchasing or contracting process. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "advertiser-workflow",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From an offer to a published listing",
        "content": "Sheypoor’s inspected help page describes a structured submission process. Advertisers add photographs, choose a category, and enter a title, description, price, and location. City and neighborhood are part of the listing information; optional settings can include contact methods. After submission, advertisers can follow the listing’s status through their account. [s2]\n\nThe same documentation distinguishes submitting an advertisement from its publication. Listings enter a review queue, and the account tools support editing and renewal. This is a moderation and publishing workflow; the existence of review does not independently establish the accuracy of every seller’s statement or guarantee the outcome of a later agreement. The help directory also separates payments, visibility promotion, violation reporting, and storefront questions, showing that maintaining an advertisement involves more than its initial submission. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-presentation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A national directory with regional emphasis",
        "content": "The homepage’s property sections distinguish buying an apartment, renting a home, and buying a villa. It highlights searches in Tehran alongside northern cities such as Sari, Babol, Amol, and Rasht, and includes a separate northern-city directory. That presentation gives northern property markets particular visibility within a broader national service. [s1]\n\nThese navigation choices are evidence of how Sheypoor organizes discovery. They are not a measurement of regional market share, listing quality, or actual sales. A city appearing prominently on the homepage cannot establish how many households use the service there, and the availability of a category does not demonstrate that every locality has equivalent supply. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "business-model-and-measurement",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Paid features and the limits of growth claims",
        "content": "Pomegranate Investment’s annual report for the period ending April 2024 describes Sheypoor as a general classifieds platform with particular regional and category strengths, including property. It reports the addition of paid features and a decline in total users and sessions during Solar Hijri 1402, alongside stronger revenue performance in a business segment. This is a shareholder’s account and should be attributed accordingly. [s3]\n\nThe combination is instructive: audience growth and revenue growth are separate questions. A platform can obtain more income from paid services while recording fewer visits or users. Neither measure alone establishes whether customers completed more satisfactory transactions. The inspected material does not support a continuous annual series with stable definitions, so it is more accurate to preserve this dated business context than to manufacture a trend from unrelated audience claims. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
